diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic pass — L2/infrastructure (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot), routing/control-plane (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot), and service/resolution (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) — found no fault anywhere in the topology.

Affected component: none identified. All subsystems are healthy.

Mechanism: The user reported that web2.local and web3.local "page just spins and never loads" from host_1_1_1_1, while web0 and web1 were fine. This pattern would be consistent with either a DNS mis-record, a crashed or unreachable web_server_2/3, an ACL blocking those specific IPs, or a routing black-hole for 10.200.0.5–6. None of these mechanisms are present.

Localization: infra_sweep found no ACL rules, no addressing anomalies, no resolver misconfiguration on any device. l2_snapshot confirmed no duplicate MACs. ospf_snapshot shows all six FRR routers with Full adjacencies and 10 OSPF routes each. tc_snapshot shows clean fq_codel on all 30 interfaces with no shaping. service_snapshot shows host_1_1_1_1 resolving web2.local → 10.200.0.5 and web3.local → 10.200.0.6 correctly, and receiving HTTP 200 from both with ~4 ms response times. pressure_sweep found no resource pressure on any device.

Root cause: No anomaly exists in the current lab state; the reported symptom cannot be reproduced and leaves no mechanism or historical trace.

Fix: No corrective action is required. The network infrastructure and all web services are operating correctly. The user's issue may have been transient (e.g., a browser or client-side cache problem, a momentary DNS lookup failure that self-resolved) or was not reproducible in the current observation window.
=== END ===